[{:db/id 17592186157235 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-0d410e49-d4f5-55b7-a3f6-063935c55bfd" "question-a78ff0f6-57a5-5952-a9ea-65ceb61c3575" "question-3d53ba03-d2db-5ca1-93a3-c5b7fe5ecc36" "question-27a0c14e-ebcc-5c2f-8221-848aff0bd39b" "question-838da398-ab9b-51bd-b33e-d5677f36abd0"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157020 :question/problem "Let $A=\\{1, 2\\}$ and consider the relation \"is greater than or equal to\" $(\\geq)$ on $A.$ If this relation is given by the set $R,$ express $R$ as a set of ordered pairs." :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A = A^2.$\n\n$$\nR\\subseteq A^2\n$$\n\nIn this case, $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y) \\in  A^2$ such that $x\\geq y.$\n\nLet's check each element $x \\in A$ to see which elements $y \\in A$ it could be paired with to satisfy the relation.\n\n- If $x=1,$ then the value $y=1$ satisfies $x\\geq y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(1,1).$\n\n- If $x=2,$ then the values $y=1$ and $y=2$ satisfy $x\\geq y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(2,1)$ and $(2,2).$\n\nTherefore, we can describe our relation as the following set of ordered pairs:\n\n$$\nR = \\{ (1,1), (2, 1), ( 2, 2) \\}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157236 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ea035c4b-8c95-55ec-bda8-6412f4cfc534" "question-e361298d-a398-53c4-959a-8c4c1fccbe8b" "question-bb1a702e-c18f-5fe7-b6f2-5f1506bc9396" "question-574049a2-4cdf-559c-928c-2935ffb5f29c" "question-fde29981-bedc-59e2-aa69-f2b0b112afe3"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157021 :question/problem "Let $A=\\{-2, 0, 2\\}$ and consider the relation \"are congruent modulo $4$\" on $A.$ Write out $R$ as a set of ordered pairs." :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A = A^2.$\n\n$$\nR\\subseteq A^2\n$$\n\nIn this case, $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y) \\in A^2$ such that $x \\equiv y \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:4).$\n\nLet's check each element $x \\in A$ to see which elements $y \\in A$ it could be paired with to satisfy the relation.\n\n- If $x=-2,$ then the values $y=-2$ and $y=2$ satisfy $x \\equiv y  \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:4),$ since $4 \\mid (-2+2)$ and $4 \\mid (-2-2).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(-2,-2)$ and $(-2,2).$\n\n- If $x=0,$ then the value $y=0$ satisfies $x \\equiv y  \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:4),$ since $4 \\mid (0-0).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(0,0).$\n\n- If $x=2,$ then the values $y=-2$ and $y=2$ satisfy $x \\equiv y  \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:4),$ since $4 \\mid (2+2)$ and $4 \\mid (2-2).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(2,-2)$ and $(2,2).$\n\nTherefore, we can describe our relation as the following set of ordered pairs:\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (-2,-2), (-2,2), (0,0), (2,-2), (2,2)\\big\\}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157237 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e3479154-8b81-59b7-8f1a-b3765342f61c" "question-2006d399-9a69-519f-8bb4-45f612aefe6e" "question-fbdd486a-767d-532e-973f-5e22a4525b04" "question-77fae439-40b7-5f50-94ed-61df5583b377" "question-5fbac889-73d0-5b8f-a301-da4ef8cb327a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157022 :question/problem "Consider the following set:\n\n$$\nA = \\big\\{0, 1\\big\\}\n$$\n\nGiven the relation $R$ on $A$ defined by\n\n$$\nx\\:R\\: y \\quad \\Leftrightarrow \\quad x\\neq y,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $0\\: R\\: 0$\n\n- $0 \\: R\\: 1$\n\n- $1 \\: \\cancel{\\!R\\!}\\: 1$" :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A = A^2.$\n\n$$\nR\\subseteq A^2\n$$\n\nWe also have the following notations:\n\n- $x \\: R \\: y$ if and only if $(x,y) \\in R$\n\n- $x \\: \\cancel{\\!R\\!} \\: y$ if and only if $(x,y) \\notin R$\n\nIn this case, we have $x \\: R \\: y$ if and only if $x\\neq y.$ For this relation, note that\n\n- the symbols $R$ and $\\neq$ are interchangeable, and\n\n- the symbols $\\cancel{\\!R\\!}$ and $=$ are also interchangeable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $\\neq$, we have $0 \\neq 0,$ which is false.\n\n- Statement II is true. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $\\neq,$ we have $0 \\neq 1,$ which is true.\n\n- Statement III is true. Replacing $\\cancel{\\!R\\!}$ with the symbol $=,$ we have $1 = 1,$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157238 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-3851f98e-30d8-57c7-a741-0f30315f4e40" "question-e8f12d3f-cce2-55c4-a847-238022b586e2" "question-c4d6f6f1-1f94-593d-9a86-a74c8d692551" "question-e334acc3-1257-55fa-a023-43a93b22b41c" "question-83c912bb-3acb-52a2-b07a-063ab598ee02"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157019 :question/problem "Let $S = \\{ 3, 5 \\}.$ Which set is equivalent to the following relation on $S?$\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\in S^2 \\: : \\: y \\not \\mid 3x \\big\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A = A^2.$\n\n$$\nR\\subseteq A\\times A\n$$\n\nIn this case, $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y) \\in S^2$ such that $y  \\not \\mid 3x .$\n\nLet's check each element $x \\in S$ to see which elements $y \\in S$ it could be paired with to satisfy the conditions of our relation.\n\n- If $x=3,$ then the value $y=5$ satisfies $y  \\not\\mid 3x.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(3,5).$\n\n- If $x=5,$  then there is no value of  $y \\in B$ that satisfies $y  \\not\\mid 3x.$\n\nTherefore, we can describe our relation as the following set of ordered pairs:\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (3,5) \\big\\}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-0d410e49-d4f5-55b7-a3f6-063935c55bfd" :question/id #uuid "0d410e49-d4f5-55b7-a3f6-063935c55bfd" :question/math-academy-id "q-212073" :question/problem "Let $A={1,2,3}$ and consider the relation \"is smaller than\" $(<)$ on $A.$ If this relation is given by the set $R,$ express $R$ as a set of ordered pairs.\n\n$R={(1,2),(1,$\n{{field-1}}$),($\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$)}$" :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in {A}^{2}$ such that $x<y.$\n\nLet's check each element $x\\in A$ to see which elements $y\\in A$ it could be paired with to satisfy the relation.\n\n- If $x=1,$ then the values $y=2$ and $y=3$ satisfy $x<y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(1,2),(1,3).$\n\n- If $x=2,$ then the value $y=3$ satisfies $x<y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(2,3).$\n\n- If $x=3,$ then there is no value of  $y\\in A$ that satisfies $x<y.$\n\nTherefore, we can describe our relation as the following set of ordered pairs:\n\n$$\nR={(1,2),(1,3),(2,3)}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-563805de-8b3a-5464-bcca-999500e33b1c" "answer-field-d86a9c9b-e486-55a1-b0ac-a61fced21ce5" "answer-field-7fc40920-1ae1-5234-8dd8-1a5fb656854a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-563805de-8b3a-5464-bcca-999500e33b1c" :answer-field/id #uuid "563805de-8b3a-5464-bcca-999500e33b1c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-c5de33ec-5eb2-57e5-a79c-a3e57a87a5b3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c5de33ec-5eb2-57e5-a79c-a3e57a87a5b3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c5de33ec-5eb2-57e5-a79c-a3e57a87a5b3" :answer/id #uuid "c5de33ec-5eb2-57e5-a79c-a3e57a87a5b3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d86a9c9b-e486-55a1-b0ac-a61fced21ce5" :answer-field/id #uuid "d86a9c9b-e486-55a1-b0ac-a61fced21ce5" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-755a51b0-9b5d-5c66-b02d-25d90741165f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-755a51b0-9b5d-5c66-b02d-25d90741165f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-755a51b0-9b5d-5c66-b02d-25d90741165f" :answer/id #uuid "755a51b0-9b5d-5c66-b02d-25d90741165f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7fc40920-1ae1-5234-8dd8-1a5fb656854a" :answer-field/id #uuid "7fc40920-1ae1-5234-8dd8-1a5fb656854a" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-77e616b4-72e5-5db8-b77f-08c2c3921e34" :answer-field/choices ["answer-77e616b4-72e5-5db8-b77f-08c2c3921e34"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-77e616b4-72e5-5db8-b77f-08c2c3921e34" :answer/id #uuid "77e616b4-72e5-5db8-b77f-08c2c3921e34" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a78ff0f6-57a5-5952-a9ea-65ceb61c3575" :question/id #uuid "a78ff0f6-57a5-5952-a9ea-65ceb61c3575" :question/math-academy-id "q-212672" :question/problem "Let $A={2,3,5}$ and consider the relation $R$  that expresses \"divides\" on $A.$ If this relation is given by the set $R,$ express $R$ as a set of ordered pairs.\n\n$R={(2,$\n{{field-1}}$),(3,$\n{{field-2}}$),($\n{{field-3}}$,$\n{{field-4}}$)}$" :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in {A}^{2}$ such that $x∣y.$\n\nLet's check each element $x\\in A$ to see which elements $y\\in A$ it could be paired with to satisfy the relation.\n\n- If $x=2,$ then the value $y=2$ satisfies $x∣y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(2,2).$\n\n- If $x=3,$ then the value $y=3$ satisfies $x∣y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(3,3).$\n\n- If $x=5,$ then the value $y=5$ satisfies $x∣y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(5,5).$\n\nTherefore we have\n\n$$\nR={(2,2),(3,3),(5,5)}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-03e9b06f-bd4f-5509-98fd-59432b430286" "answer-field-c48068cb-9159-5fa7-aa78-c179e23fc43a" "answer-field-55b2d8ba-b976-5c0b-accd-e2226b4b6ea9" "answer-field-fa977fd4-3715-5eee-bc40-a52f0c7898ad"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-03e9b06f-bd4f-5509-98fd-59432b430286" :answer-field/id #uuid "03e9b06f-bd4f-5509-98fd-59432b430286" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-f056a79a-0a00-59d3-b16e-a180d3b5faaf" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f056a79a-0a00-59d3-b16e-a180d3b5faaf"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f056a79a-0a00-59d3-b16e-a180d3b5faaf" :answer/id #uuid "f056a79a-0a00-59d3-b16e-a180d3b5faaf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c48068cb-9159-5fa7-aa78-c179e23fc43a" :answer-field/id #uuid "c48068cb-9159-5fa7-aa78-c179e23fc43a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-1e7f0681-d145-5ec9-8cb3-b4d55f818ed3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1e7f0681-d145-5ec9-8cb3-b4d55f818ed3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1e7f0681-d145-5ec9-8cb3-b4d55f818ed3" :answer/id #uuid "1e7f0681-d145-5ec9-8cb3-b4d55f818ed3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-55b2d8ba-b976-5c0b-accd-e2226b4b6ea9" :answer-field/id #uuid "55b2d8ba-b976-5c0b-accd-e2226b4b6ea9" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-4cf8994e-951f-5b63-bf5f-36a9d7822dde" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4cf8994e-951f-5b63-bf5f-36a9d7822dde"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4cf8994e-951f-5b63-bf5f-36a9d7822dde" :answer/id #uuid "4cf8994e-951f-5b63-bf5f-36a9d7822dde" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fa977fd4-3715-5eee-bc40-a52f0c7898ad" :answer-field/id #uuid "fa977fd4-3715-5eee-bc40-a52f0c7898ad" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-817425b0-ace1-5205-9154-099c68553a12" :answer-field/choices ["answer-817425b0-ace1-5205-9154-099c68553a12"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-817425b0-ace1-5205-9154-099c68553a12" :answer/id #uuid "817425b0-ace1-5205-9154-099c68553a12" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3d53ba03-d2db-5ca1-93a3-c5b7fe5ecc36" :question/id #uuid "3d53ba03-d2db-5ca1-93a3-c5b7fe5ecc36" :question/math-academy-id "q-212662" :question/problem "Let $A={0,4,5}$ and consider the relation \"is equal to\" $(=)$ on $A.$  If this relation is given by the set $R,$ express $R$ as a set of ordered pairs.\n\n$R={(0,0),(4,$\n{{field-1}}$),($\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$)}$" :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in {A}^{2}$ such that $x=y.$\n\nLet's check each element $x\\in A$ to see which elements $y\\in A$ it could be paired with to satisfy the relation.\n\n- If $x=0,$ then the value $y=0$ satisfies $x=y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(0,0).$\n\n- If $x=4,$ then the value $y=4$ satisfies $x=y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(4,4).$\n\n- If $x=5,$ then the value $y=5$ satisfies $x=y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(5,5).$\n\nTherefore, we can describe our relation as the following set of ordered pairs:\n\n$$\nR={(0,0),(4,4),(5,5)}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cb89d379-fa2f-5a85-96b3-2cc4ced981d4" "answer-field-f061e5ea-3298-55a4-9377-e3f6be2f4109" "answer-field-e4e45220-d597-524c-83e5-31b20d817060"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cb89d379-fa2f-5a85-96b3-2cc4ced981d4" :answer-field/id #uuid "cb89d379-fa2f-5a85-96b3-2cc4ced981d4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-2dd04c2b-bbc5-5101-8257-ac99a88366e5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2dd04c2b-bbc5-5101-8257-ac99a88366e5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2dd04c2b-bbc5-5101-8257-ac99a88366e5" :answer/id #uuid "2dd04c2b-bbc5-5101-8257-ac99a88366e5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f061e5ea-3298-55a4-9377-e3f6be2f4109" :answer-field/id #uuid "f061e5ea-3298-55a4-9377-e3f6be2f4109" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-1051535d-950d-5394-b25e-445ffff8656a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1051535d-950d-5394-b25e-445ffff8656a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1051535d-950d-5394-b25e-445ffff8656a" :answer/id #uuid "1051535d-950d-5394-b25e-445ffff8656a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e4e45220-d597-524c-83e5-31b20d817060" :answer-field/id #uuid "e4e45220-d597-524c-83e5-31b20d817060" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-7bffe33b-68b3-5c77-8718-5f29ce5b2417" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7bffe33b-68b3-5c77-8718-5f29ce5b2417"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7bffe33b-68b3-5c77-8718-5f29ce5b2417" :answer/id #uuid "7bffe33b-68b3-5c77-8718-5f29ce5b2417" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-27a0c14e-ebcc-5c2f-8221-848aff0bd39b" :question/id #uuid "27a0c14e-ebcc-5c2f-8221-848aff0bd39b" :question/math-academy-id "q-212668" :question/problem "Let $A={-1,1}$ and consider the relation \"is not equal to\" $(\\ne )$ on $A.$ Which of the following sets describes the relation?" :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in {A}^{2}$ such that $x\\ne y.$\n\nLet's check each element $x\\in A$ to see which elements $y\\in A$ it could be paired with to satisfy the relation.\n\n- If $x=-1,$ then the value $y=1$ satisfies $x\\ne y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(-1,1).$\n\n- If $x=1,$ then the value $y=-1$ satisfies $x\\ne y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(1,-1).$\n\nTherefore, we can describe our relation as the following set of ordered pairs:\n\n$$\nR={(-1,1),(1,-1)}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5bce2b8a-2a35-542a-8d39-426bc0f65991"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5bce2b8a-2a35-542a-8d39-426bc0f65991" :answer-field/id #uuid "5bce2b8a-2a35-542a-8d39-426bc0f65991" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a528ef20-f3df-5a73-abfe-b6f48641ed44" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7ff5ecee-cd3a-535f-b7a3-800070bca446" "answer-66a180fd-4f9d-5162-86fd-4bcd2b980abe" "answer-0779fdc3-adde-5db9-bd2f-058c400a1fe9" "answer-a528ef20-f3df-5a73-abfe-b6f48641ed44" "answer-c0fb0e19-1e92-54d0-8a47-e9675c5fc5d2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7ff5ecee-cd3a-535f-b7a3-800070bca446" :answer/id #uuid "7ff5ecee-cd3a-535f-b7a3-800070bca446" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(-1,-1),(-1,1),(1,-1),(1,1)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-66a180fd-4f9d-5162-86fd-4bcd2b980abe" :answer/id #uuid "66a180fd-4f9d-5162-86fd-4bcd2b980abe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(-1,-1),(1,1)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0779fdc3-adde-5db9-bd2f-058c400a1fe9" :answer/id #uuid "0779fdc3-adde-5db9-bd2f-058c400a1fe9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(-1,1),(1,-1),(1,1)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a528ef20-f3df-5a73-abfe-b6f48641ed44" :answer/id #uuid "a528ef20-f3df-5a73-abfe-b6f48641ed44" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(-1,1),(1,-1)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c0fb0e19-1e92-54d0-8a47-e9675c5fc5d2" :answer/id #uuid "c0fb0e19-1e92-54d0-8a47-e9675c5fc5d2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(-1,1)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-838da398-ab9b-51bd-b33e-d5677f36abd0" :question/id #uuid "838da398-ab9b-51bd-b33e-d5677f36abd0" :question/math-academy-id "q-85997" :question/problem "Let $A={1,2,3}$ and consider the relation \"is equal to\" $(=)$ on $A.$ Which of the following sets describes the relation?" :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in {A}^{2}$ such that $x=y.$\n\nLet's check each element $x\\in A$ to see which elements $y\\in A$ it could be paired with to satisfy the relation.\n\n- If $x=1,$ then the value $y=1$ satisfies $x=y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(1,1).$\n\n- If $x=2,$ then the value $y=2$ satisfies $x=y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(2,2).$\n\n- If $x=3,$ then the value $y=3$ satisfies $x=y.$  So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(3,3).$\n\nTherefore, we can describe our relation as the following set of ordered pairs:\n\n$$\nR={(1,1),(2,2),(3,3)}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-aa624be9-63b4-5b08-ab97-1c06e571369d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-aa624be9-63b4-5b08-ab97-1c06e571369d" :answer-field/id #uuid "aa624be9-63b4-5b08-ab97-1c06e571369d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9b1ed781-65d2-51f6-b0c0-b747ad7f2fb0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9b1ed781-65d2-51f6-b0c0-b747ad7f2fb0" "answer-28fffde8-0f15-590b-b313-a615bbc7d100" "answer-50e8c12d-d76f-554c-95cf-22755b1a8087" "answer-d0d08f05-09bc-57d5-a523-a34df8911c1e" "answer-594fb071-71f3-54b0-9e54-8f6f7d06c463"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9b1ed781-65d2-51f6-b0c0-b747ad7f2fb0" :answer/id #uuid "9b1ed781-65d2-51f6-b0c0-b747ad7f2fb0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(1,1),(2,2),(3,3)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-28fffde8-0f15-590b-b313-a615bbc7d100" :answer/id #uuid "28fffde8-0f15-590b-b313-a615bbc7d100" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(1,1),(2,2)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-50e8c12d-d76f-554c-95cf-22755b1a8087" :answer/id #uuid "50e8c12d-d76f-554c-95cf-22755b1a8087" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(1,2),(2,3),(3,1)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d0d08f05-09bc-57d5-a523-a34df8911c1e" :answer/id #uuid "d0d08f05-09bc-57d5-a523-a34df8911c1e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(1,2)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-594fb071-71f3-54b0-9e54-8f6f7d06c463" :answer/id #uuid "594fb071-71f3-54b0-9e54-8f6f7d06c463" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(1,3),(1,2),(2,3)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ea035c4b-8c95-55ec-bda8-6412f4cfc534" :question/id #uuid "ea035c4b-8c95-55ec-bda8-6412f4cfc534" :question/math-academy-id "q-212994" :question/problem "Let $A={1,5,8,10}$ and consider the relation \"are congruent modulo $6$\" on $A.$ Write out $R$ as a set of ordered pairs." :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in {A}^{2}$ such that $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6).$\n\nLet's check each element $x\\in A$ to see which elements $y\\in A$ it could be paired with to satisfy the relation.\n\n- If $x=1,$ then the value $y=1$ satisfies $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6),$ since $6∣(1-1).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(1,1).$\n\n- If $x=5,$ then the value $y=5$ satisfies $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6),$ since $6∣(5-5).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(5,5).$\n\n- If $x=8,$ then the value $y=8$ satisfies $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6),$ since $6∣(8-8).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(8,8).$\n\n- If $x=10,$ then the value $y=10$ satisfies $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6),$ since $6∣(10-10).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(10,10).$\n\nTherefore, we can describe our relation as the following set of ordered pairs:\n\n$$\nR={(1,1),(5,5),(8,8),(10,10)}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6d607400-fd49-5439-8653-e1c05d6d24d1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6d607400-fd49-5439-8653-e1c05d6d24d1" :answer-field/id #uuid "6d607400-fd49-5439-8653-e1c05d6d24d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c88518ac-e616-52fd-9a8b-b836755a0e56" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fa7fff05-0e03-5f11-9307-fb11de4611ab" "answer-c88518ac-e616-52fd-9a8b-b836755a0e56" "answer-15dac1d9-fe63-5721-9ab9-da8fc1bab8f2" "answer-2f2b76e5-878a-50c1-800f-9991b9c884ac" "answer-18ee8622-5b6f-58a5-805d-8e60c7ef123e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fa7fff05-0e03-5f11-9307-fb11de4611ab" :answer/id #uuid "fa7fff05-0e03-5f11-9307-fb11de4611ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(1,1),(10,1)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c88518ac-e616-52fd-9a8b-b836755a0e56" :answer/id #uuid "c88518ac-e616-52fd-9a8b-b836755a0e56" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(1,1),(5,5),(8,8),(10,10)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-15dac1d9-fe63-5721-9ab9-da8fc1bab8f2" :answer/id #uuid "15dac1d9-fe63-5721-9ab9-da8fc1bab8f2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(1,1),(5,5),(8,8)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2f2b76e5-878a-50c1-800f-9991b9c884ac" :answer/id #uuid "2f2b76e5-878a-50c1-800f-9991b9c884ac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(1,5),(5,1),(8,8)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-18ee8622-5b6f-58a5-805d-8e60c7ef123e" :answer/id #uuid "18ee8622-5b6f-58a5-805d-8e60c7ef123e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(1,8),(5,10),(8,1),(10,5)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e361298d-a398-53c4-959a-8c4c1fccbe8b" :question/id #uuid "e361298d-a398-53c4-959a-8c4c1fccbe8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-212996" :question/problem "Let $A={2,5}$ and consider the relation \"are congruent modulo $3$\" on $A.$ Write out $R$ as a set of ordered pairs." :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in {A}^{2}$ such that $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3).$\n\nLet's check each element $x\\in A$ to see which elements $y\\in A$ it could be paired with to satisfy the relation.\n\n- If $x=2,$ then the values $y=2$ and $y=5$ satisfy $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3),$ since $3∣(2-2)$ and $3∣(2-5).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(2,2),(2,5).$\n\n- If $x=5,$ then the values $y=2$ and $y=5$ satisfy $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3),$ since $3∣(5-2)$ and $3∣(5-5).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(5,2),(5,5).$\n\nTherefore, we can describe our relation as the following set of ordered pairs:\n\n$$\nR={(2,2),(2,5),(5,2),(5,5)}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-87aa18b4-cff3-56a1-bf1b-654c57d19070"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-87aa18b4-cff3-56a1-bf1b-654c57d19070" :answer-field/id #uuid "87aa18b4-cff3-56a1-bf1b-654c57d19070" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-05d68007-c8a5-5b35-9d67-46ee6326aaf8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-05d68007-c8a5-5b35-9d67-46ee6326aaf8" "answer-781984fa-07a6-5b6f-b98d-17c1d0d8bee3" "answer-a6537ffb-bb0e-5640-97f1-20f818f26f43" "answer-fc102a2a-5763-5d46-8b6b-bbee3d064deb" "answer-772a90b0-1a02-5e16-b0a0-a46a4c8a84c0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-05d68007-c8a5-5b35-9d67-46ee6326aaf8" :answer/id #uuid "05d68007-c8a5-5b35-9d67-46ee6326aaf8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(2,2),(2,5),(5,2),(5,5)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-781984fa-07a6-5b6f-b98d-17c1d0d8bee3" :answer/id #uuid "781984fa-07a6-5b6f-b98d-17c1d0d8bee3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(2,2),(2,5),(5,5)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a6537ffb-bb0e-5640-97f1-20f818f26f43" :answer/id #uuid "a6537ffb-bb0e-5640-97f1-20f818f26f43" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(2,2),(5,2),(5,5)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fc102a2a-5763-5d46-8b6b-bbee3d064deb" :answer/id #uuid "fc102a2a-5763-5d46-8b6b-bbee3d064deb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(2,2),(5,5)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-772a90b0-1a02-5e16-b0a0-a46a4c8a84c0" :answer/id #uuid "772a90b0-1a02-5e16-b0a0-a46a4c8a84c0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(2,5),(5,2)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-bb1a702e-c18f-5fe7-b6f2-5f1506bc9396" :question/id #uuid "bb1a702e-c18f-5fe7-b6f2-5f1506bc9396" :question/math-academy-id "q-213003" :question/problem "Consider the following set:\n\n$$\nA={{0},{1,0}}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following ordered pairs is an element of the relation \"is **not** a subset of\" on $A?$ \n\n- $({0},{1,0})$\n- $({1,0},{0})$\n- $({1,0},{1,0})$" :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in {A}^{2}$ such that $x⊈y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our ordered pairs in turn.\n\n- The ordered pair $({0},{1,0})$ is **not** an element of $R$ since ${0}⊆{1,0}.\\,\\times $\n\n- The ordered pair $({1,0},{0})$ is an element of $R$ since ${1,0}⊈{0}.\\,✓$\n\n- The ordered pair $({1,0},{1,0})$ is **not** an element of $R$ since ${1,0}⊆{1,0}.\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cee0f5da-3466-5854-93e2-01adf980becc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cee0f5da-3466-5854-93e2-01adf980becc" :answer-field/id #uuid "cee0f5da-3466-5854-93e2-01adf980becc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-046a0b5d-b969-58cc-947c-f4f868bbbdde" :answer-field/choices ["answer-83250d56-27d9-5d35-998f-7c225e26de93" "answer-f5797c74-f7f7-51f3-9dfa-2fc0175bef87" "answer-d0ffa5ba-9fc3-5ce6-841a-f2088a84936d" "answer-046a0b5d-b969-58cc-947c-f4f868bbbdde" "answer-1c321d12-faed-5b6b-b6cb-b31615bfec34"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-83250d56-27d9-5d35-998f-7c225e26de93" :answer/id #uuid "83250d56-27d9-5d35-998f-7c225e26de93" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f5797c74-f7f7-51f3-9dfa-2fc0175bef87" :answer/id #uuid "f5797c74-f7f7-51f3-9dfa-2fc0175bef87" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d0ffa5ba-9fc3-5ce6-841a-f2088a84936d" :answer/id #uuid "d0ffa5ba-9fc3-5ce6-841a-f2088a84936d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-046a0b5d-b969-58cc-947c-f4f868bbbdde" :answer/id #uuid "046a0b5d-b969-58cc-947c-f4f868bbbdde" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1c321d12-faed-5b6b-b6cb-b31615bfec34" :answer/id #uuid "1c321d12-faed-5b6b-b6cb-b31615bfec34" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the ordered pairs are elements of the relation." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-574049a2-4cdf-559c-928c-2935ffb5f29c" :question/id #uuid "574049a2-4cdf-559c-928c-2935ffb5f29c" :question/math-academy-id "q-212993" :question/problem "Let $A={2,5,7,8}$ and consider the relation \"are congruent modulo $4$\" on $A.$ Write out $R$ as a set of ordered pairs." :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in {A}^{2}$ such that $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4).$\n\nLet's check each element $x\\in A$ to see which elements $y\\in A$ it could be paired with to satisfy the relation.\n\n- If $x=2,$ then the value $y=2$ satisfies $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4),$ since $4∣(2-2).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(2,2).$\n\n- If $x=5,$ then the value $y=5$ satisfies $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4),$ since $4∣(5-5).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(5,5).$\n\n- If $x=7,$ then the value $y=7$ satisfies $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4),$ since $4∣(7-7).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(7,7).$\n\n- If $x=8,$ then the value $y=8$ satisfies $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4),$ since $4∣(8-8).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(8,8).$\n\nTherefore, we can describe our relation as the following set of ordered pairs:\n\n$$\nR={(2,2),(5,5),(7,7),(8,8)}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c1dc1273-46be-5e0f-ac29-ed393ac36d5f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c1dc1273-46be-5e0f-ac29-ed393ac36d5f" :answer-field/id #uuid "c1dc1273-46be-5e0f-ac29-ed393ac36d5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f13b87d5-60ca-51a7-a731-e812b70eabc3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f13b87d5-60ca-51a7-a731-e812b70eabc3" "answer-ac6d453c-2244-5dae-b520-317dbb2b7fdd" "answer-693664ab-3d6c-5886-bbe2-793b4383b88b" "answer-f6a44e7a-ffa7-553f-91c8-4409f1dfdae6" "answer-4f234402-5996-5684-928e-7b0ae18f1678"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f13b87d5-60ca-51a7-a731-e812b70eabc3" :answer/id #uuid "f13b87d5-60ca-51a7-a731-e812b70eabc3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(2,2),(5,5),(7,7),(8,8)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ac6d453c-2244-5dae-b520-317dbb2b7fdd" :answer/id #uuid "ac6d453c-2244-5dae-b520-317dbb2b7fdd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(2,2),(5,5),(7,7)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-693664ab-3d6c-5886-bbe2-793b4383b88b" :answer/id #uuid "693664ab-3d6c-5886-bbe2-793b4383b88b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(2,2),(8,2)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f6a44e7a-ffa7-553f-91c8-4409f1dfdae6" :answer/id #uuid "f6a44e7a-ffa7-553f-91c8-4409f1dfdae6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(2,5),(5,2),(7,7)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4f234402-5996-5684-928e-7b0ae18f1678" :answer/id #uuid "4f234402-5996-5684-928e-7b0ae18f1678" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(2,7),(5,8),(7,2),(8,5)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-fde29981-bedc-59e2-aa69-f2b0b112afe3" :question/id #uuid "fde29981-bedc-59e2-aa69-f2b0b112afe3" :question/math-academy-id "q-212992" :question/problem "Let $A={2,6,7}$ and consider the relation \"are congruent modulo $3$\" on $A.$ Write out $R$ as a set of ordered pairs." :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in {A}^{2}$ such that $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3).$\n\nLet's check each element $x\\in A$ to see which elements $y\\in A$ it could be paired with to satisfy the relation.\n\n- If $x=2,$ then the value $y=2$ satisfies $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3),$ since $3∣(2-2).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(2,2).$\n\n- If $x=6,$ then the value $y=6$ satisfies $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3),$ since $3∣(6-6).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(6,6).$\n\n- If $x=7,$ then the value $y=7$ satisfies $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3),$ since $3∣(7-7).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(7,7).$\n\nTherefore, we can describe our relation as the following set of ordered pairs:\n\n$$\nR={(2,2),(6,6),(7,7)}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3df79f96-c8f5-54fb-8485-e7041ea3278a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3df79f96-c8f5-54fb-8485-e7041ea3278a" :answer-field/id #uuid "3df79f96-c8f5-54fb-8485-e7041ea3278a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a93c8df8-be46-5d45-9c8b-528320c106ee" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2968b5a9-37c2-509f-ae03-8610194ddb2a" "answer-350f986b-683b-5497-b64f-269441499b3e" "answer-a93c8df8-be46-5d45-9c8b-528320c106ee" "answer-81b0727d-ee68-559d-8314-8b52ff89305c" "answer-0b23e99e-1de5-5692-a3a2-5e71190c3a00"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2968b5a9-37c2-509f-ae03-8610194ddb2a" :answer/id #uuid "2968b5a9-37c2-509f-ae03-8610194ddb2a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(2,2),(2,6),(6,2),(6,6),(7,7)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-350f986b-683b-5497-b64f-269441499b3e" :answer/id #uuid "350f986b-683b-5497-b64f-269441499b3e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(2,2),(2,7),(6,6),(7,2),(7,7)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a93c8df8-be46-5d45-9c8b-528320c106ee" :answer/id #uuid "a93c8df8-be46-5d45-9c8b-528320c106ee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(2,2),(6,6),(7,7)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-81b0727d-ee68-559d-8314-8b52ff89305c" :answer/id #uuid "81b0727d-ee68-559d-8314-8b52ff89305c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(2,2),(6,6)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0b23e99e-1de5-5692-a3a2-5e71190c3a00" :answer/id #uuid "0b23e99e-1de5-5692-a3a2-5e71190c3a00" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(2,2),(7,7)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e3479154-8b81-59b7-8f1a-b3765342f61c" :question/id #uuid "e3479154-8b81-59b7-8f1a-b3765342f61c" :question/math-academy-id "q-213145" :question/problem "Consider the following set:\n\n$$\nA={1,2}\n$$\n\nGiven the relation $R$ on $A$ defined by\n\n$$\nx\\,R\\,y\\,⇔\\,x∣y,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $1\\,R\\,1$\n- $1\\,R\\,2$\n- $2\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,2$" :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nWe also have the following notations:\n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\in R$\n\n- $x\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\notin R$\n\nIn this case, we have $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x∣y.$ For this relation, note that\n\n- the symbols $R$ and $∣$ are interchangeable, and\n\n- the symbols $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ and $∤$ are also interchangeable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $∣$, we have $1∣1,$ which is true.\n\n- Statement II is true. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $∣,$ we have $1∣2,$ which is true.\n\n- Statement III is false. Replacing $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ with the symbol $∤,$ we have $2∤2,$ which is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5deadd58-de08-5459-bea5-ad5b2c6a1739"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5deadd58-de08-5459-bea5-ad5b2c6a1739" :answer-field/id #uuid "5deadd58-de08-5459-bea5-ad5b2c6a1739" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6fb48062-390e-5f50-95e5-46245a4669c8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6fb48062-390e-5f50-95e5-46245a4669c8" "answer-8868f605-fc4c-52a6-a072-da3b71bc8621" "answer-df350fa9-2809-583c-a268-f8b8d3e8def1" "answer-d231e4f4-a503-5b71-aee4-7e628eb0ceaf" "answer-862baade-6c89-5f3f-a85b-756cfdc43c56"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6fb48062-390e-5f50-95e5-46245a4669c8" :answer/id #uuid "6fb48062-390e-5f50-95e5-46245a4669c8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8868f605-fc4c-52a6-a072-da3b71bc8621" :answer/id #uuid "8868f605-fc4c-52a6-a072-da3b71bc8621" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-df350fa9-2809-583c-a268-f8b8d3e8def1" :answer/id #uuid "df350fa9-2809-583c-a268-f8b8d3e8def1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d231e4f4-a503-5b71-aee4-7e628eb0ceaf" :answer/id #uuid "d231e4f4-a503-5b71-aee4-7e628eb0ceaf" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-862baade-6c89-5f3f-a85b-756cfdc43c56" :answer/id #uuid "862baade-6c89-5f3f-a85b-756cfdc43c56" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2006d399-9a69-519f-8bb4-45f612aefe6e" :question/id #uuid "2006d399-9a69-519f-8bb4-45f612aefe6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-213141" :question/problem "Consider the following set:\n\n$$\nA={{0},{0,1}}\n$$\n\nGiven the relation $R$ on $A$ defined by\n\n$$\nX\\,R\\,Y\\,⇔\\,X⊃Y,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${0}\\,R\\,{0,1}$\n- ${0,1}\\,R\\,{0,1}$\n- ${0}\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,{0}$" :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊂{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nWe also have the following notations:\n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\in R$\n\n- $x\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\notin R$\n\nIn this case, we have $X\\,R\\,Y$ if and only if $X⊃Y.$ For this relation, note that\n\n- the symbols $R$ and $⊃$ are interchangeable, and\n\n- the symbols $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ and $⊅$ are also interchangeable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $⊃,$ we have ${0}⊃{0,1}.$ This is equivalent to ${0,1}⊂{0},$ which is false.\n\n- Statement II is false. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $⊃,$ we have ${0,1}⊃{0,1}.$ This is equivalent to ${0,1}⊂{0,1},$ which is false.\n\n- Statement III is true. Replacing $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ with the symbol $⊅,$ we have ${0}⊅{0}.$ This is equivalent to ${0}⊄{0},$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b5e8b322-e012-50ae-b918-cd16e0ce7f37"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b5e8b322-e012-50ae-b918-cd16e0ce7f37" :answer-field/id #uuid "b5e8b322-e012-50ae-b918-cd16e0ce7f37" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a68b31a5-13d7-58c4-8b9a-c44045ea0cd4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-50d4ef47-2283-5013-800d-55532771b590" "answer-8488ff21-a2ea-5b43-889c-08cc212c8dc2" "answer-94a8c259-c943-5b5a-8249-ef29a64409c8" "answer-a68b31a5-13d7-58c4-8b9a-c44045ea0cd4" "answer-69a086d9-ed8a-5d19-a711-31dea503c803"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-50d4ef47-2283-5013-800d-55532771b590" :answer/id #uuid "50d4ef47-2283-5013-800d-55532771b590" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8488ff21-a2ea-5b43-889c-08cc212c8dc2" :answer/id #uuid "8488ff21-a2ea-5b43-889c-08cc212c8dc2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-94a8c259-c943-5b5a-8249-ef29a64409c8" :answer/id #uuid "94a8c259-c943-5b5a-8249-ef29a64409c8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a68b31a5-13d7-58c4-8b9a-c44045ea0cd4" :answer/id #uuid "a68b31a5-13d7-58c4-8b9a-c44045ea0cd4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-69a086d9-ed8a-5d19-a711-31dea503c803" :answer/id #uuid "69a086d9-ed8a-5d19-a711-31dea503c803" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-fbdd486a-767d-532e-973f-5e22a4525b04" :question/id #uuid "fbdd486a-767d-532e-973f-5e22a4525b04" :question/math-academy-id "q-212638" :question/problem "Consider the following set:\n\n$$\nA={e,\\pi }\n$$\n\nGiven the relation $R$ on $A$ defined by\n\n$$\nx\\,R\\,y\\,⇔\\,x\\ne y,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $e\\,R\\,e$\n- $e\\,R\\,\\pi $\n- $\\pi \\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,\\pi $" :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nWe also have the following notations:\n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\in R$\n\n- $x\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\notin R$\n\nIn this case, we have $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x\\ne y.$ For this relation, note that\n\n- the symbols $R$ and $\\ne $ are interchangeable, and\n\n- the symbols $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ and $=$ are also interchangeable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $\\ne $, we have $e\\ne e,$ which is false.\n\n- Statement II is true. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $\\ne ,$ we have $e\\ne \\pi ,$ which is true.\n\n- Statement III is true. Replacing $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ with the symbol $=,$ we have $\\pi =\\pi ,$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a9c640c9-599a-5e63-a2a4-02ba3cb94fbb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a9c640c9-599a-5e63-a2a4-02ba3cb94fbb" :answer-field/id #uuid "a9c640c9-599a-5e63-a2a4-02ba3cb94fbb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-fd3906e4-2bc8-51c1-b715-a02b38473796" :answer-field/choices ["answer-256131e7-1b92-545f-8144-d6cc2b71dbde" "answer-f21c9568-89f5-550c-aeca-3135fd1e4015" "answer-fd3906e4-2bc8-51c1-b715-a02b38473796" "answer-03cf3dd2-1d1e-571a-b1f1-4ac972be63d2" "answer-744c2bf9-2be2-5a86-9e71-395056b3825f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-256131e7-1b92-545f-8144-d6cc2b71dbde" :answer/id #uuid "256131e7-1b92-545f-8144-d6cc2b71dbde" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f21c9568-89f5-550c-aeca-3135fd1e4015" :answer/id #uuid "f21c9568-89f5-550c-aeca-3135fd1e4015" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fd3906e4-2bc8-51c1-b715-a02b38473796" :answer/id #uuid "fd3906e4-2bc8-51c1-b715-a02b38473796" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-03cf3dd2-1d1e-571a-b1f1-4ac972be63d2" :answer/id #uuid "03cf3dd2-1d1e-571a-b1f1-4ac972be63d2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-744c2bf9-2be2-5a86-9e71-395056b3825f" :answer/id #uuid "744c2bf9-2be2-5a86-9e71-395056b3825f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-77fae439-40b7-5f50-94ed-61df5583b377" :question/id #uuid "77fae439-40b7-5f50-94ed-61df5583b377" :question/math-academy-id "q-213144" :question/problem "Consider the following set:\n\n$$\nA={2,4,6}\n$$\n\nGiven the relation $R$ on $A$ defined by\n\n$$\nx\\,R\\,y\\,⇔\\,x∤y,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $2\\,R\\,4$\n- $4\\,R\\,6$\n- $2\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,6$" :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nWe also have the following notations:\n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\in R$\n\n- $x\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\notin R$\n\nIn this case, we have $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x∤y.$ For this relation, note that\n\n- the symbols $R$ and $∤$ are interchangeable, and\n\n- the symbols $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ and $∣$ are also interchangeable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $∤$, we have $2∤4,$ which is false.\n\n- Statement II is true. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $∤,$ we have $4∤6,$ which is true.\n\n- Statement III is true. Replacing $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ with the symbol $∣,$ we have $2∣6,$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ce8e8452-662e-5d96-814f-930fa128fbd9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ce8e8452-662e-5d96-814f-930fa128fbd9" :answer-field/id #uuid "ce8e8452-662e-5d96-814f-930fa128fbd9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d26ca1a3-71a4-578b-b77f-cb0242ad66b4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-db9ed992-4a83-51d6-999e-2f07e0541fbd" "answer-239771eb-1873-5866-a462-6727c30f35e5" "answer-4b2c6f96-fc4e-5564-95d5-c89c5d1f7d0a" "answer-d26ca1a3-71a4-578b-b77f-cb0242ad66b4" "answer-f7a71d76-19a3-532b-ba22-51c4aa5690b4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-db9ed992-4a83-51d6-999e-2f07e0541fbd" :answer/id #uuid "db9ed992-4a83-51d6-999e-2f07e0541fbd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-239771eb-1873-5866-a462-6727c30f35e5" :answer/id #uuid "239771eb-1873-5866-a462-6727c30f35e5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4b2c6f96-fc4e-5564-95d5-c89c5d1f7d0a" :answer/id #uuid "4b2c6f96-fc4e-5564-95d5-c89c5d1f7d0a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d26ca1a3-71a4-578b-b77f-cb0242ad66b4" :answer/id #uuid "d26ca1a3-71a4-578b-b77f-cb0242ad66b4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f7a71d76-19a3-532b-ba22-51c4aa5690b4" :answer/id #uuid "f7a71d76-19a3-532b-ba22-51c4aa5690b4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5fbac889-73d0-5b8f-a301-da4ef8cb327a" :question/id #uuid "5fbac889-73d0-5b8f-a301-da4ef8cb327a" :question/math-academy-id "q-213127" :question/problem "Consider the following set:\n\n$$\nA={1,3}\n$$\n\nGiven the relation $R$ on $A$ defined by\n\n$$\nx\\,R\\,y\\,⇔\\,x=y,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $1\\,R\\,1$\n- $1\\,R\\,3$\n- $3\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,3$" :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nWe also have the following notations:\n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\in R$\n\n- $x\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\notin R$\n\nIn this case, we have $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x=y.$ For this relation, note that\n\n- the symbols $R$ and $=$ are interchangeable, and\n\n- the symbols $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ and $\\ne $ are also interchangeable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is  true. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $=$, we have $1=1,$ which is true.\n\n- Statement II is false. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $=,$ we have $1=3,$ which is false.\n\n- Statement III is false. Replacing $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ with the symbol $\\ne ,$ we have $3\\ne 3,$ which is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6eed9603-46c9-5a60-91ed-069b81019517"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6eed9603-46c9-5a60-91ed-069b81019517" :answer-field/id #uuid "6eed9603-46c9-5a60-91ed-069b81019517" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7a3dd6bc-4ebf-5239-844d-55920fd64463" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f09ba51b-bb92-5a76-b8d4-9b2465b49c33" "answer-de98701f-4772-5961-b734-93e00e6687bc" "answer-7a3dd6bc-4ebf-5239-844d-55920fd64463" "answer-2d51ed98-ac24-5fc6-a3ec-b6f3ee2ab3da" "answer-843aa144-29e1-5a7a-b5ed-0a15c64b58f2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f09ba51b-bb92-5a76-b8d4-9b2465b49c33" :answer/id #uuid "f09ba51b-bb92-5a76-b8d4-9b2465b49c33" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-de98701f-4772-5961-b734-93e00e6687bc" :answer/id #uuid "de98701f-4772-5961-b734-93e00e6687bc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7a3dd6bc-4ebf-5239-844d-55920fd64463" :answer/id #uuid "7a3dd6bc-4ebf-5239-844d-55920fd64463" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2d51ed98-ac24-5fc6-a3ec-b6f3ee2ab3da" :answer/id #uuid "2d51ed98-ac24-5fc6-a3ec-b6f3ee2ab3da" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-843aa144-29e1-5a7a-b5ed-0a15c64b58f2" :answer/id #uuid "843aa144-29e1-5a7a-b5ed-0a15c64b58f2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3851f98e-30d8-57c7-a741-0f30315f4e40" :question/id #uuid "3851f98e-30d8-57c7-a741-0f30315f4e40" :question/math-academy-id "q-212987" :question/problem "Let $B={2,3,4}.$ Which set is equivalent to the following relation on $B?$\n\n$$\nR={(x,y)\\in {B}^{2}\\,:\\,6∣xy}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆A\\times A\n$$\n\nIn this case, $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in {B}^{2}$ such that $6∣xy.$\n\nLet's check each element $x\\in B$ to see which elements $y\\in B$ it could be paired with to satisfy the conditions of our relation.\n\n- If $x=2,$ then the value $y=3$ satisfies $6∣xy.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(2,3).$\n\n- If $x=3,$ then the values $y=2,4$ satisfy $6∣xy.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(3,2)$ and $(3,4).$\n\n- If $x=4,$ then the value $y=3$ satisfies $6∣xy.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(4,3).$\n\nTherefore, we can describe our relation as the following set of ordered pairs:\n\n$$\nR={(2,3),(3,2),(3,4),(4,3)}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-bdb7ef21-56fd-5217-8d74-98919d28940a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-bdb7ef21-56fd-5217-8d74-98919d28940a" :answer-field/id #uuid "bdb7ef21-56fd-5217-8d74-98919d28940a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-049c5d72-e52b-5919-9ea2-e45de1d3dc93" :answer-field/choices ["answer-653be230-d17e-59ac-bd89-f763870e1489" "answer-049c5d72-e52b-5919-9ea2-e45de1d3dc93" "answer-edd90124-3f30-5b6b-88a0-76519c0384f9" "answer-f9611862-6363-5312-95fd-1bd8b5529014" "answer-fc327c96-f859-56ca-8311-44d9c34d34ca"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-653be230-d17e-59ac-bd89-f763870e1489" :answer/id #uuid "653be230-d17e-59ac-bd89-f763870e1489" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(2,2),(3,2),(3,4),(4,3),(4,4)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-049c5d72-e52b-5919-9ea2-e45de1d3dc93" :answer/id #uuid "049c5d72-e52b-5919-9ea2-e45de1d3dc93" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(2,3),(3,2),(3,4),(4,3)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-edd90124-3f30-5b6b-88a0-76519c0384f9" :answer/id #uuid "edd90124-3f30-5b6b-88a0-76519c0384f9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(2,3),(3,2)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f9611862-6363-5312-95fd-1bd8b5529014" :answer/id #uuid "f9611862-6363-5312-95fd-1bd8b5529014" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(2,3),(3,4)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fc327c96-f859-56ca-8311-44d9c34d34ca" :answer/id #uuid "fc327c96-f859-56ca-8311-44d9c34d34ca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(3,4),(4,3)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e8f12d3f-cce2-55c4-a847-238022b586e2" :question/id #uuid "e8f12d3f-cce2-55c4-a847-238022b586e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-85991" :question/problem "Let $B={0,1,2,3,4}.$ Which set is equivalent to the following relation on $B?$\n\n$$\nR={(x,y)\\in {B}^{2}\\,:\\,y=2x}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆A\\times A\n$$\n\nIn this case, $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in {B}^{2}$ such that $y=2x.$\n\nLet's check each element $x\\in B$ to see which elements $y\\in B$ it could be paired with to satisfy the conditions of our relation.\n\n- If $x=0,$  then the value $y=0$ satisfies $y=2x.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(0,0).$\n\n- If $x=1,$ then the value $y=2$ satisfies $y=2x.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair  $(1,2).$\n\n- If $x=2,$ then the value $y=4$ satisfies $y=2x.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair  $(2,4).$\n\n- If $x=3,$  then there is no value of $y\\in B$ that satisfies $y=2x.$\n\n- If $x=4,$ then there is no value of $y\\in B$ that satisfies $y=2x.$\n\nTherefore, we can describe our relation as the following set of ordered pairs:\n\n$$\nR={(0,0),(1,2),(2,4)}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-02cbbdcb-3772-5ead-be55-a689a2b35c64"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-02cbbdcb-3772-5ead-be55-a689a2b35c64" :answer-field/id #uuid "02cbbdcb-3772-5ead-be55-a689a2b35c64" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5f3e4d2b-6935-5acd-bca1-e452ceaf0a64" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9db20c0f-75fd-5a68-95fc-a43ea8d75d14" "answer-25fea826-58a1-557d-b3b1-f039d3b38948" "answer-5f3e4d2b-6935-5acd-bca1-e452ceaf0a64" "answer-bec92dfe-fed6-5699-95d9-4104be6385c5" "answer-bc145f72-ba17-5e60-a29a-392f841ec3c6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9db20c0f-75fd-5a68-95fc-a43ea8d75d14" :answer/id #uuid "9db20c0f-75fd-5a68-95fc-a43ea8d75d14" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(0,0),(1,2),(2,1)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-25fea826-58a1-557d-b3b1-f039d3b38948" :answer/id #uuid "25fea826-58a1-557d-b3b1-f039d3b38948" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(0,0),(1,2),(2,4),(3,6),(4,8)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5f3e4d2b-6935-5acd-bca1-e452ceaf0a64" :answer/id #uuid "5f3e4d2b-6935-5acd-bca1-e452ceaf0a64" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(0,0),(1,2),(2,4)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bec92dfe-fed6-5699-95d9-4104be6385c5" :answer/id #uuid "bec92dfe-fed6-5699-95d9-4104be6385c5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(0,1),(1,2),(3,4)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bc145f72-ba17-5e60-a29a-392f841ec3c6" :answer/id #uuid "bc145f72-ba17-5e60-a29a-392f841ec3c6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(2,2),(4,4)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c4d6f6f1-1f94-593d-9a86-a74c8d692551" :question/id #uuid "c4d6f6f1-1f94-593d-9a86-a74c8d692551" :question/math-academy-id "q-80827" :question/problem "Let $X={-3,4}.$ Which set is equivalent to the following relation on $X?$\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\in {X}^{2}\\,:\\,x>y-2}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆A\\times A\n$$\n\nIn this case, $S$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in {X}^{2}$ such that $x>y-2.$\n\nLet's check each element $x\\in X$ to see which elements $y\\in X$ it could be paired with to satisfy the conditions of our relation.\n\n- If $x=-3,$ then the value $y=-3$ satisfies $x>y-2.$ So, $S$ contains the ordered pair $(-3,-3).$\n\n- If $x=4,$ then the values $y=-3,4$ satisfy $x>y-2.$ So, $S$ contains the ordered pairs $(4,-3),(4,4).$\n\nTherefore, we can describe our relation as the following set of ordered pairs:\n\n$$\nS={(-3,-3),(4,-3),(4,4)}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0371d26b-89a5-595b-af82-53817cdb9826"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0371d26b-89a5-595b-af82-53817cdb9826" :answer-field/id #uuid "0371d26b-89a5-595b-af82-53817cdb9826" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-03d2e1d8-b19e-57f6-97a6-97712408191a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-aa395104-66ea-531c-86a0-d4e5a9926b19" "answer-03d2e1d8-b19e-57f6-97a6-97712408191a" "answer-b7fc812e-bc00-5c66-92c5-c5fcbf5ecbab" "answer-2f1a5510-01d0-517e-aa7d-b577a1995fee" "answer-4088dcdd-6da7-565d-a4c7-f6c2fdec1349"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aa395104-66ea-531c-86a0-d4e5a9926b19" :answer/id #uuid "aa395104-66ea-531c-86a0-d4e5a9926b19" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(-3,-3),(-3,4),(4,-3),(4,4)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-03d2e1d8-b19e-57f6-97a6-97712408191a" :answer/id #uuid "03d2e1d8-b19e-57f6-97a6-97712408191a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(-3,-3),(4,-3),(4,4)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b7fc812e-bc00-5c66-92c5-c5fcbf5ecbab" :answer/id #uuid "b7fc812e-bc00-5c66-92c5-c5fcbf5ecbab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(-3,-3),(4,4),}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2f1a5510-01d0-517e-aa7d-b577a1995fee" :answer/id #uuid "2f1a5510-01d0-517e-aa7d-b577a1995fee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(3,3),(4,4)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4088dcdd-6da7-565d-a4c7-f6c2fdec1349" :answer/id #uuid "4088dcdd-6da7-565d-a4c7-f6c2fdec1349" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(4,-3)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e334acc3-1257-55fa-a023-43a93b22b41c" :question/id #uuid "e334acc3-1257-55fa-a023-43a93b22b41c" :question/math-academy-id "q-212682" :question/problem "Let $B={-1,0,1}.$ Which set is equivalent to the following relation on $B?$\n\n$$\nR={(x,y)\\in {B}^{2}\\,:\\,|x|\\ne |y|}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆A\\times A\n$$\n\nIn this case, $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in {B}^{2}$ such that $|x|\\ne |y|.$\n\nLet's check each element $x\\in B$ to see which elements $y\\in B$ it could be paired with to satisfy the conditions of our relation.\n\n- If $x=-1,$  then the value $y=0$ satisfies $|x|\\ne |y|.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(-1,0).$\n\n- If $x=0,$ then the values $y=-1$ and $y=1$ satisfy $|x|\\ne |y|.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(0,-1)$ and $(0,1).$\n\n- If $x=1,$  then the value $y=0$ satisfies $|x|\\ne |y|.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(1,0).$\n\nTherefore, we can describe our relation as the following set of ordered pairs:\n\n$$\nR={(-1,0),(0,-1),(0,1),(1,0)}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c992d4ef-f4b0-5356-b57f-ca3d4886f15e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c992d4ef-f4b0-5356-b57f-ca3d4886f15e" :answer-field/id #uuid "c992d4ef-f4b0-5356-b57f-ca3d4886f15e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ed06703d-b1da-5c2e-baee-e17baa87d3f2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-062eef9a-d695-51df-997b-6dbff43970c5" "answer-ed06703d-b1da-5c2e-baee-e17baa87d3f2" "answer-a5a23483-f183-50bb-b8a0-25abcc57fd99" "answer-9b310337-8e8d-5fcf-b163-488e852a4756" "answer-0cc4d213-ab46-5a61-9eeb-6fcdea273470"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-062eef9a-d695-51df-997b-6dbff43970c5" :answer/id #uuid "062eef9a-d695-51df-997b-6dbff43970c5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(-1,-1),(0,0),(1,1)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ed06703d-b1da-5c2e-baee-e17baa87d3f2" :answer/id #uuid "ed06703d-b1da-5c2e-baee-e17baa87d3f2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(-1,0),(0,-1),(0,1),(1,0)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a5a23483-f183-50bb-b8a0-25abcc57fd99" :answer/id #uuid "a5a23483-f183-50bb-b8a0-25abcc57fd99" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(-1,0),(0,1)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9b310337-8e8d-5fcf-b163-488e852a4756" :answer/id #uuid "9b310337-8e8d-5fcf-b163-488e852a4756" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(-1,0),(1,0)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0cc4d213-ab46-5a61-9eeb-6fcdea273470" :answer/id #uuid "0cc4d213-ab46-5a61-9eeb-6fcdea273470" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(0,-1),(0,1)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-83c912bb-3acb-52a2-b07a-063ab598ee02" :question/id #uuid "83c912bb-3acb-52a2-b07a-063ab598ee02" :question/math-academy-id "q-212680" :question/problem "Let $B={2,4}.$ Which set is equivalent to the following relation on $B?$\n\n$$\nR={(x,y)\\in {B}^{2}\\,:\\,y\\ne 2x}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆A\\times A\n$$\n\nIn this case, $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in {B}^{2}$ such that $y\\ne 2x.$\n\nLet's check each element $x\\in B$ to see which elements $y\\in B$ it could be paired with to satisfy the conditions of our relation.\n\n- If $x=2,$  then the value $y=2$ satisfies $y\\ne 2x.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(2,2).$\n\n- If $x=4,$ then the values $y=2$ and $y=4$ satisfy $y\\ne 2x.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(4,2)$ and $(4,4).$\n\nTherefore, we can describe our relation as the following set of ordered pairs:\n\n$$\nR={(2,2),(4,2),(4,4)}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fecaf988-cb99-5dc9-a94d-0ddb5fc7e352"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fecaf988-cb99-5dc9-a94d-0ddb5fc7e352" :answer-field/id #uuid "fecaf988-cb99-5dc9-a94d-0ddb5fc7e352" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-611cf4d1-8a67-5f11-a534-3c447c496121" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d426d50d-255d-544f-bf06-8545672015f0" "answer-e78a8bd7-845d-5e27-bae5-2124620c8c46" "answer-611cf4d1-8a67-5f11-a534-3c447c496121" "answer-f290c802-71c9-53a5-a438-0ec774d59a21" "answer-5ff85075-84c8-552a-abcf-15d9e99ceec0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d426d50d-255d-544f-bf06-8545672015f0" :answer/id #uuid "d426d50d-255d-544f-bf06-8545672015f0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(2,2),(2,4),(4,2),(4,4)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e78a8bd7-845d-5e27-bae5-2124620c8c46" :answer/id #uuid "e78a8bd7-845d-5e27-bae5-2124620c8c46" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(2,2),(2,4),(4,4)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-611cf4d1-8a67-5f11-a534-3c447c496121" :answer/id #uuid "611cf4d1-8a67-5f11-a534-3c447c496121" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(2,2),(4,2),(4,4)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f290c802-71c9-53a5-a438-0ec774d59a21" :answer/id #uuid "f290c802-71c9-53a5-a438-0ec774d59a21" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(2,2),(4,4)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5ff85075-84c8-552a-abcf-15d9e99ceec0" :answer/id #uuid "5ff85075-84c8-552a-abcf-15d9e99ceec0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(2,4),(4,2)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186158585 :tutorial/content "We'll now begin discussing **relations**, one of the most important topics in elementary set theory. Most (if not all) of the mathematical concepts you've learned up to this point can be thought of in terms of relations. The goal is to give these concepts a common framework.\n\nTo start with, let's define the set $A$ as\n\n$$\nA = \\big\\{1,2,3,4\\big\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, consider the following standard relationships we can define between elements of $A{:}$\n\n- Suppose we want to define the concept \"$a$ is less than $b$\" over the elements of $A.$ One way to do this is to write down a set containing *all ordered* pairs $(a,b)$ such that $a < b$ and $a, b\\in A.$ This gives the following set:\n\n$$\nR_{<} = \\big\\{(1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(2,3),(2,4), (3,4)\\big\\}\n$$\n\n- Next, suppose we want to define \"$a$ divides $b$\" over the elements of $A.$ This time, we write down the ordered pairs $(a,b)$ such that $a \\mid b.$ \n\n$$\nR_{\\,\\mid\\, } = \\big\\{(1,1), (1,2), (1,3), (1,4), (2,2), (2,4),(3,3), (4,4) \\big\\}\n$$\n\n \nNote that $(1,1) \\in R_{\\,\\mid\\, }$ since $1 \\mid 1.$ The same is true for for $(2,2),(3,3),(4,4).$\n\n- Finally, let's define \"$a$ has the same parity as $b$\" over $A.$ Remember that two numbers have the same parity if they're both even or both odd: \n\n$$\nR_{p} = \\big\\{(1,1),(1,3),(2,2),(2,4),(3,1),(3,3), (4,2), (4,4)\\big\\}\n$$\n\n \nNote that \"$2$ has the same parity as $4$\" and \"$4$ has the same parity as $2$\" are considered separately. Since both are true, we include $(2,4)$ *and* $(4,2)$ in our set of ordered pairs.\n\nAlthough $R_{\\,<\\,}, R_{\\,\\mid\\,}$ and $R_p$ describe very different relationships between pairs of elements of $A,$ they have one thing in common: they're all *subsets of the Cartesian product* $A\\times A.$ This suggests that subsets of the Cartesian product can be used to describe a wide variety of mathematical relationships that exist between pairs of set elements. \n\nThis gives us our first definition:\n\n*A **binary relation** $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A = A^2.$*\n\n$$\nR\\subseteq A^2\n$$\n\nWe often refer to binary relations simply as **relations**. So, the sets $R_{\\,<\\,}, R_{\\,\\mid\\,}$ and $R_p$ are all (binary) relations on $A.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186158588 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a binary relation $R$ on $A,$ so $R\\subseteq A^2.$ Then, the following notation is often convenient:\n\n- When $(x,y) \\in R,$ we write $x \\: R \\: y.$ In words, we say \"$x$ is related to $y$ by $R.$\"\n\n- When $(x,y) \\notin R,$ we write $\\require{cancel} x \\cancel{R} y.$ In words, we say \"$x$ is *not* related to $y$ by $R.$\"\n\nFor example, consider the following relation:\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\in \\mathbb{Z}^2 \\: : \\: x \\mid y \\big\\}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe following statements are equivalent:\n\n$$\n(2,10) \\in R\n\\qquad\\Leftrightarrow\\qquad\n2 \\: R \\: 10\n$$\n\nWe also have\n\n$$\n(3,10) \\notin R\n\\qquad\\Leftrightarrow\\qquad\n3 \\: {\\cancel{\\!R\\!}} \\: 10\n$$\n\nThe relation $x \\mid y$ is a so-called **standard relation.** With standard relations, we can replace the letter $R$ with the usual symbol describing the relation to retrieve our usual notation:\n\n$$\n(2,10) \\in R \n\\qquad\\Leftrightarrow\\qquad \n2 \\: {\\color{red}{R}} \\: 10\n\\qquad\\Leftrightarrow\\qquad\n2 \\:{\\color{red}{\\mid}}\\:  10\n$$\n\nFor an ordered pair that's not an element of our relation, we negate the usual symbol.\n\n$$\n(3,10) \\not\\in R\n\\qquad\\Leftrightarrow\\qquad \n3 \\: {\\color{red}{\\cancel{\\!R\\!}}} \\: 10\n\\qquad\\Leftrightarrow\\qquad\n3 \\:{\\color{red}{\\not\\mid}}\\:  10\n$$\n\nThe same idea applies to other standard relations. For example, consider the set $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=\\big\\{2,4,6,8,10\\big\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSuppose we define the relations \"is less than\" $(\\lt)$, \"is congruent modulo $2$ to\" $(\\equiv),$ and \"is equal to\" $(=)$ over $A.$ Then, we have the following equivalences.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2,10)\\in {R}_{<} & \\,⇔\\,2\\,{R}_{<}\\,10\\, &  & ⇔\\,2<10 \\\\ (2,4)\\in {R}_{≡} & \\,⇔\\,2\\,{R}_{≡}\\,4\\, &  & ⇔\\,2≡4 \\\\ (6,6)\\in {R}_{=} & \\,⇔\\,6\\,{R}_{=}\\,6\\, &  & ⇔\\,6=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, for elements not in the standard relation, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(10,2)\\notin {R}_{<} & \\,⇔\\,10\\,\\cancel{\\,{R}_{<}\\,}\\,2\\, &  & ⇔\\,10≮2 \\\\ (1,4)\\notin {R}_{≡} & \\,⇔\\,1\\,\\cancel{\\,{R}_{≡}}\\,\\,4\\, &  & ⇔\\,1≢4 \\\\ (2,4)\\notin {R}_{=} & \\,⇔\\,2\\,\\cancel{\\,{R}_{=}\\,}\\,4\\, &  & ⇔\\,2\\ne 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, not only can relations be thought of as a general way to describe relationships between mathematical objects, but the $a\\,R\\, b$ notation is something you've been using all along!" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186158589 :tutorial/content "The relations we've considered so far (e.g., \"is greater than\" or \"divides\") are all standard relations that are encountered regularly when studying mathematics.\n\nHowever, defining a relation as a subset of a Cartesian product allows us to think of relations more broadly.\n\nTo explain, let's consider the following set:\n\n$$\nA = \\big\\{1,2,3,4\\big\\}.\n$$\n\nWe saw how to define \"$a$ is less than $b,$\" \"$a$ divides $b$,\" and \"$a$ has the same parity as $b$\" over $A.$ But relations do not necessarily need to have any \"standard\" meaning attributed to them. For example, the following set is also a valid relation on $A{:}$ \n\n$$\nR= \\big\\{(1,2), (1,4), (3,2), (4,3)\\big\\}\n$$\n\nYou might be hard-pressed to find an intuitive meaning behind this relation, but it's a relation on $A$ nonetheless! We'll refer to these kinds of relations as **non-standard relations.**\n\nWe can also define non-standard relations using set-builder notation. For example, consider the following relation over $B = \\{2,4,5\\}.$ \n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\in B^2 \\: : \\: x \\mid y^2 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y) \\in B^2$ such that $x$ divides $y^2{:}$\n\nLet's check each element $x \\in B$ to see which elements $y \\in B$ it could be paired with to satisfy the conditions of our relation.\n\n- If $x={\\color{red}{2}},$ then the values $y={\\color{blue}{2}}$ and $y={\\color{blue}{4}}$ satisfy $x\\mid y^2,$ since\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{2}}\\mid {\\color{blue}{2}}^2, \\quad \\textrm{and}\\quad {\\color{red}{2}}\\mid {\\color{blue}{4}}^2.\n$$\n\nSo, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $({\\color{red}{2}},{\\color{blue}{2}})$ and $({\\color{red}{2}},{\\color{blue}{4}}).$\n\n- If $x={\\color{red}{4}},$ then the values $y={\\color{blue}{2}}$ and $y={\\color{blue}{4}}$ satisfy $x\\mid y^2,$ since\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{4}}\\mid {\\color{blue}{2}}^2, \\quad \\textrm{and}\\quad {\\color{red}{4}}\\mid {\\color{blue}{4}}^2.\n$$\n\nSo, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $({\\color{red}{4}},{\\color{blue}{2}})$ and $({\\color{red}{4}},{\\color{blue}{4}}).$\n\n- If $x={\\color{red}{5}},$ then the value $y={\\color{blue}{5}}$ satisfies $x\\mid y^2,$ since\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{5}}\\mid {\\color{blue}{5}}^2.\n$$\n\nSo, $R$ contains the ordered pair $({\\color{red}{5}},{\\color{blue}{5}}).$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{\n({\\color{red}{2}},{\\color{blue}{2}}), ({\\color{red}{2}},{\\color{blue}{4}}), ({\\color{red}{4}},{\\color{blue}{2}}), ({\\color{red}{4}},{\\color{blue}{4}}), ({\\color{red}{5}},{\\color{blue}{5}})\n\\big\\}.\n$$\n\n**Watch Out!** When applying the $x\\,R\\,y$ notation to non-standard relations, care must be used when replacing $R$ with the symbol involved in the description of the relation. For example,\n\n$$\n({\\color{red}{4}},{\\color{blue}{2}}) \\in R \\quad \\Leftrightarrow\\quad {\\color{red}{4}}\\mid {\\color{blue}{2}}^2\n$$\n\n \n\nhowever\n\n$$\n({\\color{red}{4}},{\\color{blue}{2}}) \\in R \\quad \\not\\Leftrightarrow\\quad {\\color{red}{4}}\\mid {\\color{blue}{2}}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157475 :activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
